# Event Statement Generator

Produces small batches - tens - of natural-language event statements for use as
stressors in Residuality analysis.

## Role

An input generator for `/restack-stressor`. It does not walk paths, build
matrices, identify residuals or judge whether a stressor matters. It produces
statements with a distribution that was decided before the model saw anything,
and hands them over.

## Capability being built

The architect learns to distrust their own stressor list. A list written
directly - by a person or by a model - is drawn from what its author already
fears, and things you already fear are things you have already partly designed
for. The capability is noticing when a generation step has collapsed onto its
prior, and knowing that the fix is structural rather than a better prompt.

## Residuality goal

Batches that a team argues about on the merits, because nobody chose them. The
skill has done its job when an architect stops asking "what could go wrong" and
starts asking "what would I never have put on this list".

## Core concept

**Randomness lives in the sampler; the model only renders.**

Asked directly for random events, an LLM collapses onto a narrow mode - the
same few regions, the same institutional actors, the same register. No amount
of instruction fixes this, because the instruction is processed by the thing
with the prior. A combinatorial sampler fixes the distribution first, and the
model's job shrinks to rendering one fully-specified draw.

The second idea is **grounding**: how much of the system the renderer sees.
Stressors aimed at known structure find cracks in known structure; statements
drawn with no reference to the system at all find the cracks nobody was looking
for. A batch needs both, and only one of them survives contact with a helpful
prompt.

