## Locating the scripts (run this first)

Both scripts ship **inside this skill**, so they survive installation. Resolve
them once per session - installed location first, repo checkout as the fallback
- and use the variables for every command below:

```bash
SAMPLE="$HOME/.claude/skills/restack-events/scripts/sample.py"
[ -f "$SAMPLE" ] || SAMPLE="skills/restack-events/scripts/sample.py"
VALIDATE="$HOME/.claude/skills/restack-events/scripts/validate.py"
[ -f "$VALIDATE" ] || VALIDATE="skills/restack-events/scripts/validate.py"
[ -f "$SAMPLE" ] || echo "restack-events scripts not found - reinstall the skill"
```

The skill is used from whatever project the architect is working in, not from
the ReStack checkout. A bare relative path only resolves when the working
directory happens to be the repository.

## Commands

### 1. `/restack-events batch [n]`

Generate a full batch, end to end. Default 30, which is where
`/restack-stressor` wants a matrix.

**Step 1 - draw the specs.** Pick a seed and say what it is, so the batch can
be reproduced.

```bash
python "$SAMPLE" --n 30 --seed <seed> --out specs.jsonl
```

The default `--balance grounding,plausibility` stratifies over the nine cells,
so roughly three specs per cell and blind draws guaranteed in every batch.

**Step 2 - read `sections/render-protocol.md`.** Do not render from memory of
what the briefs probably say. The no-tools rule on the blind tracks is the part
that is easy to lose and impossible to detect afterwards.

**Step 3 - render.** One subagent per spec, launched concurrently, around eight
at a time. Each gets one spec, no conversation history, and the brief its
`grounding` value selects. **STOP** if you are about to render more than one
spec in a single subagent: the isolation is the design, not an optimisation.

**Step 4 - collect** into `statements.jsonl`, one JSON object per line:
`{"spec_id": ..., "statement": ..., "grounding": ...}`.

**Step 5 - validate.**

```bash
python "$VALIDATE" statements.jsonl --specs specs.jsonl --forbid "<system terms>"
```

`--forbid` takes the terms that identify the system under analysis - its name,
its components, its customers, its sector. Any of them appearing in an
uncoupled or adjacent statement means context reached a prompt that should not
have had it. **STOP** and re-render those specs; nothing downstream will reveal
it and the batch will look fine.

**Step 6 - report.** The seed, the cell spread, anything validation flagged,
and the statements themselves. Say plainly which came from the blind tracks.

### 2. `/restack-events specs [n]`

Step 1 only - draw specs and stop. Use when the rendering will happen later or
elsewhere, or when tuning weights and you want to see the distribution without
paying for renders.

### 3. `/restack-events validate <statements> <specs>`

Step 5 only, on a batch generated earlier.

### 4. `/restack-events tune`

Read `skills/restack-events/reference/taxonomy.json` and adjust weights against
what the last matrix showed. Weights are relative integers normalised at load;
a dimension whose values all scored alike is not discriminating. Read
`sections/handoff.md` first - it covers what to change between iterations and
why reusing a seed is not a second iteration.

The taxonomy is data, not code. Never edit `sample.py` to change a
distribution.

## Constraints

**Institutional actors only, never a named real person.** `actor_type` carries
only institutional and generic categories, deliberately. A fabricated claim
attributed to a named real person is defamatory content whatever label travels
with it, and text leaves a pipeline without its labels.

**These are hypotheticals, not claims about the world.** Nothing here asserts
that an event happened, and a batch cannot be used to measure anything about
real-world accuracy. A stressor's truth value never enters the matrix - its job
is to be walked.

**Determinism stops at the sampler.** `sample.py` is fully reproducible from a
seed; subagent rendering is not. Specs replay exactly, statements do not, so
keep `specs.jsonl` beside `statements.jsonl`.

**No API, no key, no network.** Both scripts are standard-library Python and
rendering runs on subagents inside the session. Nothing here needs
`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` or outbound access. The budget to watch is subagent turns.

